BOOTSTRAP.md:221:   The commander is not a question. It is the principal's own session in the directory whose `CLAUDE.md` you are about to write, so it exists whether or not anyone names it; it goes into `seats.json` as the `commander` entry, marked `"external": true`, because it runs on its own harness and the adapter manages nothing about it. Say that, and ask whether that commander session is launched directly or under a wrapper/status-line layer; if it is wrapped, point them at `seats/README.md`'s `WHEELHOUSE_HERALD_IDLE_RE` measurement recipe before choosing an idle regex. Then walk the rest individually — each answer is yes-with-a-count or no. Keep each prompt about its own role only: do not mention another role name inside the worker, reviewer, verifier, or designer question, because a tool-driven fixture or a human scanning quickly can classify the question by the wrong role. With `AskUserQuestion`, use labels shaped like the records you will write: `2 workers`, `1 reviewer`, `Take verifier`, `Take designer`, and matching decline labels.
BOOTSTRAP.md:406:  3. **Watching the fleet.** How dispatches go out (`bun seats/adapter.ts dispatch <seat> <bead-id> ...`) and where each seat's events land (`seats/logs/<seat>.jsonl`), with `bun seats/adapter.ts status` as the always-available liveness view. Then the bridge, by name, because it is real machinery this install just copied and a vague pointer here is the difference between a cockpit that gets used and one that gets rediscovered months in: `seats/cockpit.sh <namespace>` builds (or re-attaches to — it never duplicates, and a re-run respawns a missing floor pane) the project's tmux session `wh-<namespace>`, commander pane on the left, starts `commander-inbox-poll.sh` inside that pane as the wrapper-independent inbox wake, and `bun seats/floor.ts` on the right is the read-only floor — spotlight one seat, rail of every seat's attention cues. Write the real namespace into that line. Point at `seats/README.md`'s "The bridge" section for the keys and what each cue means rather than restating them.
